::ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Simplify 3x + 4x.
{
~A. 12x#This multiplies the coefficients instead of adding them. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
=B. 7x#Both terms are x-terms, so add the coefficients: 3 + 4 = 7.
~C. 7x²#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps the variable part x.
~D. 3x + 4#This drops the x from the second term. The original expression has 4x, so both terms are like terms.
}

::ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Simplify 8a - 3a + 2.
{
~A. 5a#This combines the a-terms but leaves out the constant. Constants remain part of the expression.
~B. 11a + 2#This adds 8a and 3a instead of subtracting 3a. Pay attention to the operation sign.
~C. 7a#This combines the constant with the variable term. Constants and variable terms are not like terms.
=D. 5a + 2#Combine 8a and -3a to get 5a, and keep the constant 2 separate.
}

::ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Rewrite 2(x + 5) using the distributive property.
{
=A. 2x + 10#Multiply 2 by x and multiply 2 by 5.
~B. 2x + 5#This multiplies 2 by x but not by 5. Distribute to every term inside the parentheses.
~C. 7x#This adds 2 and 5 and attaches x. The distributive property requires multiplication.
~D. 10x#This multiplies 2 and 5 but does not distribute to x correctly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Rewrite 4(3n - 2) using the distributive property.
{
~A. 12n - 2#This distributes 4 to 3n but not to -2. Every term inside the parentheses must be multiplied.
~B. 7n - 2#This adds 4 and 3n instead of multiplying. Distribution uses multiplication.
=C. 12n - 8#4 times 3n is 12n, and 4 times -2 is -8.
~D. 12n + 8#This changes the subtraction to addition. The sign on -2 must be kept when distributing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Simplify 3(y + 2) + y.
{
~A. 3y + 2#This distributes only to y and does not combine the extra y. Distribute first, then combine like terms.
=B. 4y + 6#3(y + 2) becomes 3y + 6, and 3y + y = 4y.
~C. 3y + 6#This distributes correctly but does not combine the additional y-term.
~D. 4y + 2#This combines y-terms but does not distribute 3 to the constant 2.
}
